  "era": "Resident Evil/Silent Hill era to Signalis revival",
  "summary": "Dithered fog, fixed cameras, polygon faces — horror born of hardware limits.",
  "description": "The PlayStation survival horror look — Resident Evil's Spencer Mansion, Silent Hill's fog-banked streets — was engineered by constraint: low-poly models, pre-rendered fixed-camera backgrounds, heavy dithering, and grain filters that hid polygon seams while adding analog dread. Fog in Silent Hill both masked draw distance and became the game's psychology; Resident Evil's static frames turned rooms into stage plays of reveal. The style's uncanny textures and limited animation read as wrongness, which is why 2020s indie horror (Signalis, Crow Country, Tormented Souls) deliberately resurrects the PS1 grain as a genre costume.",
  "culturalContext": "PS1 survival horror's constraints became horror's psychology: fog masked draw distance and became dread, fixed cameras turned rooms into stage plays, and the grain aesthetic now signals genre in indie revivals (Signalis, Crow Country). The look is gaming's most complete proof that limitation breeds style.",
